\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measurement and monitoring of poverty are central to assessing global
 development progress.
 For the World Bank, whose mission is to eradicate extreme poverty on a
 livable planet, the ability to track poverty reduction is fundamental for
 measuring institutional effectiveness and guiding policy decisions.
 Yet, a persistent challenge hampers this crucial task: the limited availability
 of recent, high-quality household survey data that includes information
 that allows for comparable measures of expenditure or income across time.
 This challenge is particularly acute in countries where poverty is most
 concentrated 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "DangJolliffeCarletto,worldbank2024poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The issue affects many countries, and hinders monetary poverty monitoring
 due to the lack of data in some of the world's most populous and poverty
 afflicted economies.
 For example, India and Nigeria account for a substantial share of the world's
 poor, meaning that they heavily influence global poverty trends.
 In India, official consumption survey data was unavailable between 2011
 and 2022.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The 75th round of the NSO Survey of Consumption Expenditure was collected
 in 2017-18 but was not released until after 2024: 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "https://www.mospi.gov.in/unit-level-data-report-nss-75th-round-july-2017-june-2018-schedule-250social-consumption-health"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In Nigeria comparable survey data for poverty monitoring was absent between
 2009 and 2018, and as of 2025 the country has no new publicly available
 consumption survey data.
 These data gaps not only affect our understanding of poverty at the country
 level but compromise our ability to produce reliable global poverty estimates
 and assess progress toward poverty reduction goals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Traditional poverty measurement relies on household surveys that collect
 detailed consumption or income data.
 These surveys represent substantial investments in both financial and human
 resources.
 They require extensive preparation, careful implementation, and can place
 considerable burden on responding households, who must either maintain
 detailed consumption diaries or participate in comprehensive recall interviews.
 The method of data collection itself can introduce significant biases into
 poverty estimates which may compromise comparability of estimates over
 time.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For instance, consumption diaries – while theoretically more accurate –
 can lead to respondent fatigue and underreporting over time.
 Recall modules, on the other hand, may suffer from memory bias, with longer
 recall periods typically resulting in lower reported consumption.
 Research has shown that simply changing the recall period or the number
 of consumption items can lead to substantially different poverty estimates
 within the same population (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "beegle2012methods"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 These methodological challenges add another layer of complexity to the
 already demanding task of poverty measurement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The challenges of collecting traditional living standards surveys for poverty
 measurement become particularly acute during crises when standard survey
 operations are disrupted or impossible to conduct.
 Armed conflicts, natural disasters, health emergencies, and other humanitarian
 crises often prevent face-to-face household interviews precisely when poverty
 monitoring becomes most crucial.
 The COVID-19 pandemic provided a stark illustration of this challenge,
 as household survey efforts were halted globally at a time when understanding
 welfare impacts was most critical.
 Similar data collection constraints arise in conflict zones, where security
 concerns prevent enumerator access to households, or during natural disasters
 that displace populations and disrupt statistical operations.
 During such crises, policymakers and international organizations must resort
 to alternative methods for estimating poverty.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Survey-to-survey (S2S) imputation has emerged as a methodological response
 to these data limitations.
 The method is often used as a way to produce poverty numbers when traditional
 welfare data collection is unavailable due to costs, complexity, conflict,
 or other reasons.
 S2S, often combined with rapid phone surveys or other alternative data
 collection methods, has emerged as a key tool for maintaining poverty monitorin
g during these challenging periods where traditional surveys cannot be collected.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
S2S methods build upon techniques originally developed for small area estimation
 (SAE) in poverty mapping, pioneered by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and further refined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These imputation methods enable poverty estimation using surveys that lack
 direct welfare measures by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Developing a predictive model of household welfare using a survey with consumpti
on or income data (the "source" survey) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Applying this model to a different survey containing similar household character
istics but lacking direct welfare (the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

target
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 survey)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Generating poverty estimates based on the predicted welfare distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While S2S and small area estimation share methodological foundations, they
 serve distinct purposes.
 Unit-level small area estimation of poverty, as developed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and advanced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, typically applies nationally estimated models (from a household survey)
 to population census data to generate precise poverty estimates for small
 geographic areas where survey estimates are often too imprecise for reliable
 estimates.
 In contrast, S2S focuses on applying the model to a separate survey to
 predict welfare, regardless of geographic disaggregation.
 One might view traditional poverty mapping as a special case of S2S where
 the target datasets are the different areas of the census and the primary
 goal is obtaining geographically disaggregated estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although a potentially an attractive solution, S2S approaches come with
 their own limitations and potential biases that must be carefully considered.
 This paper first examines the limitations and potential pitfalls of survey-to-s
urvey imputation through rigorous analysis of its fundamental assumptions.
 This is a technical paper that relies on simulated data to conduct experiments.
 In these experiments, the focus is not on traditional concerns such as
 variable selection or model specification, but rather on the method's core
 limitations, particularly for measuring poverty across extended time periods
 or different populations.
 The analysis relies on simulated data to isolate and demonstrate specific
 mechanisms that can generate biased estimates.
 This approach allows for clear illustration of three critical findings:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Standard sampling bias-correction techniques, such as re-weighting to match
 population means, may be insufficient when source and target surveys differ
 fundamentally.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The method's tendency to replicate the welfare distribution of the source
 survey can make it unreliable for measuring changes in inequality and poverty.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The model captures the joint distribution between 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in the source data.
 The parameters corresponding to the source data are then applied to the
 target data, where the only difference is the distribution of covariates,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, unless all the change is captured by changes in the covariates the
 method will likely give biased estimates.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Thus, better communication behind the limitations of the methods is crucial.
 These insights are particularly relevant given the increasing reliance
 on survey-to-survey imputation to fill data gaps in poverty monitoring.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Third, omitted variable bias will likely affect poverty predictions, particularl
y when imputing across time periods that include significant economic shocks
 or structural changes.
 This last finding has important implications for applications that use
 "fast-moving" variables to capture welfare changes,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "SwiftMethod"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 refer to expenditure related covariates as fast moving.
 These include dichotomous variables indicating if a household reported
 purchasing a product or not.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 as these variables may introduce bias if they are correlated with unobserved
 factors that affect welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Through systematic examination of these limitations using simplified examples,
 the paper provides practitioners with a framework for understanding when
 and why S2S methods can fail.
 The results here are of relevance since survey-to-survey imputation has
 become more prevalent in recent times to overcome data scarcity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
T One example is the multiple applications in India, where survey data useful
 for global poverty monitoring have not been released since 2011.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The effectiveness of S2S across time: A summary of the evidence 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Survey-to-Survey-Imputation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Estimating poverty relies on household survey data that collects household
 consumption or income.
 Collecting household consumption and income data is costly and complex.
 For consumption, modules typically span multiple domains—food, housing,
 health, education, and more—each requiring detailed recall and careful
 categorization.
 These surveys are usually fielded over a full year to represent seasonal
 consumption patterns.
 As 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "deaton1998consumption"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 observe, these modules are among the most demanding components of household
 surveys, often encompassing hundreds of items and multiple recall periods.
 This not only increases respondent burden but also drives up implementation
 costs.
 Ensuring accuracy adds another layer of complexity: respondents may forget
 or misreport expenditures, and cultural and regional differences often
 require localized adaptations in survey design.
 Moreover, as 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "beegle2012methods"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show, even small changes in how surveys are administered can yield significant
 differences in reported poverty and consumption levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because of the complexity and cost, few countries collect comparable consumption
 or income data frequently.
 Long-term, harmonized consumption trends are the exception, not the norm.
 Yet, policymakers, donors, and development practitioners continually need
 up-to-date poverty statistics.
 To fill this gap, survey-to-survey imputation has become an increasingly
 used method for estimating poverty when consumption or income data are
 unavailable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
S2S builds upon the idea proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro,jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 where a linear regression model is fit on a survey where consumption or
 income are available to determine the joint distribution of consumption
 or income, and a given set of household characteristics – this survey is
 typically referred to as the source or training data.
 The model parameters are then applied to a separate dataset (the target
 data) where the same set of household characteristics can be found with
 the aim of replicating the welfare distribution (consumption or income)
 via imputation.
 With a simulated welfare distribution in hand, poverty estimates and other
 welfare indicators can be derived from the target data.
 The methods can be applied to contemporaneous surveys or to surveys correspondi
ng to a different point in time.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
S2S is useful when applied to contemporaneous surveys to obtain statistics
 across the welfare distribution for indicators that are not available in
 the living standards survey.
 For example, the Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) provide a litany
 of health related indicators that are not available in most living standards
 surveys that collect welfare for poverty estimation.
 This is where S2S comes into play.
 A model trained on the living standards survey and applied to the DHS can
 help us inform about the difference in stunting rates between poor and
 non-poor households or determine how it varies across the welfare distribution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The method is often treated as a prediction exercise and unlike much of
 econometrics, the parameters of the linear regression are not intended
 to capture only the direct effect of the characteristics on welfare (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, p356).
 Nevertheless, the original intent of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 was to predict on to the same population.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When imputing to a different year, the method rests on the assumption that
 model parameters remain stable—that is, the relationship between household
 characteristics and welfare has not changed.
 In other words, the method assumes that model parameters remain stationary
 over time – meaning that any observed changes in poverty are solely attributabl
e to changes in the model's covariates, rather than shifts in unobservable
 factors or changing returns to these covariates (Dang, Lanjouw and Serajuddin
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citebyear
key "dang2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 As 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "christiaensen2012small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 note, such assumptions may be especially problematic in rapidly growing
 economies, where structural economic changes can alter the relationship
 between poverty and its predictors.
 It can also be problematic when a strong shock has occurred between the
 survey periods – for example, a global pandemic or changes in a country's
 fragility.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mathiassen2009model"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 emphasizes that the validity of imputations rests on a strong assumption
 of parameter stability and explicitly warns against extrapolating too far
 across time or applying the method when significant changes in welfare
 dynamics or survey design are likely.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Researchers and practitioners have proposed various approaches to address
 the limitations of S2S in predicting poverty over time.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "stifel2007tracking"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 advocate for including time-varying variables such as rainfall and prices
 to capture temporal changes.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "SwiftMethod"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 suggest incorporating variables that track economic conditions more directly,
 like indicators on whether the household consumed a given good.
 However, as 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "SwiftMethod"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 emphasize, without updated training data to re-estimate model parameters,
 the risk of missing significant economic changes remains substantial, even
 with these additional covariates.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dang2025poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 further note that evidence on the utility of item-specific consumption
 indicators (e.g., expenditures or quantities for particular goods) remains
 inconclusive, and instead advocate for parsimonious models that are easier
 to implement in surveys and in challenging field conditions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The effectiveness of S2S imputation across time has been tested in a range
 of contexts, with mixed results.
 For example, Dang, Lanjouw and Serajuddin (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citebyear
key "dang2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) evaluate S2S using Jordan’s Household Expenditure and Income Survey and
 the Employment and Unemployment Survey; 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "stifel2007tracking"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 conduct similar tests for Kenya; and Dang et al.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citebyear
key "dang2025poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) apply the method across multiple countries—including Ethiopia, Malawi,
 Nigeria, Tanzania, and Viet Nam—reporting estimates generally within acceptable
 error margins.
 While some studies report estimates within acceptable margins of error
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "dang2025poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "https://doi.org/10.1111/roiw.12007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), others find significant discrepancies between predicted and observed
 poverty rates (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "newhouse2014survey"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "https://doi.org/10.1111/roiw.12007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 A useful example comes from Malawi, where 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mathiassen2021predicting"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 document their efforts to produce comparable poverty estimates between
 2004 and 2014.
 While their method performed well within individual surveys and with careful
 seasonal adjustment, comparability across survey rounds proved fragile.
 Shifts in survey design and implementation such as shorter questionnaires,
 periods of data collection, changing field protocols, and others led to
 unstable predictor relationships and inconsistent poverty trends over time.
 Even in this relatively well-structured case, the authors conclude that
 S2S imputation should only be used when comparability of predictors and
 survey instruments can be assured (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "mathiassen2021predicting"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "christiaensen2012small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 undertake an empirical validation of survey-to-survey imputation methods
 over time.
 The authors perform survey-to-survey imputation in scenarios where there
 is comparable expenditure data which provides a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 estimate of poverty.
 The authors validate their approach with data for Viet Nam and for China
 using rural household panel data.
 In Viet Nam, the authors obtain a model using the 1992/93 data and predict
 poverty using the 1997/98 data.
 They note that the method works relatively well and depending on the covariates
 used, differences between predicted and observed poverty rates were on
 average 3.4 percentage points during a period where poverty fell by 23.2
 percentage points.
 For the Chinese regions where the method was tested, the authors also find
 that the methods work relatively well.
 However, depending on the model used, differences between predicted and
 observed rates were considerable.
 This highlights the method's conundrum: different models may generate different
 findings, and at least some models may get it right.
 However, ex-ante, it is very difficult to determine which model will yield
 the most accurate estimates or even the right estimates.
 So far, no foolproof methods or diagnostics exist to reliably identify
 the ideal model, raising fundamental concerns about the robustness of imputed
 poverty estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applications of survey-to-survey (S2S) imputation have also made their way
 into global poverty monitoring—for example, until recently, every poverty
 number for India after 2011 was based on an imputation.
 However, evidence from real-world applications, particularly when time
 gaps are large or covariate distributions change substantially, reveals
 mixed findings and highlights the need for caution.
 These findings underscore the importance of understanding the context and
 data very well when applying S2S across different periods and economic
 environments.
 To better appreciate how such estimates are produced and the assumptions
 they rest on, the next section outlines the foundations of survey-to-survey
 imputation, tracing its origins in the broader field of missing data methodolog
ies and clarifying its application in the context of poverty measurement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The basics behind survey-to-survey imputation (S2S)
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:The-basics-behind"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imputation is a method for filling in missing data.
 According to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, the first instance of a statistical method to replace a missing value
 dates to 1930,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "allan1930method"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and the first widespread use of the term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

imputation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 comes from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "madow1983incomplete"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As noted by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The method was originally proposed to fill in missing observations and
 considered the nature of the missing data (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "dempster1977maximum"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 The goal of multiple imputation is not to create a single prediction, but
 rather multiple imputations to reflect the uncertainty around the actual
 value (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 By generating plausible values for missing data, multiple imputation allows
 for valid estimation and the construction of appropriate confidence intervals
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For example, in the case of a regression on agricultural yields, the variable
 capturing plot sizes may have missing values and these must be imputed
 with the aim of not losing information as well as obtaining a valid estimate
 of the coefficient for the relationship between land and yields.
 Using multiple imputation in this example, as opposed to just the predicted
 land size, is expected to reduce the rate of false positives.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Type I errors, which occur when a null hypothesis is incorrectly rejected.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The case of a variable that is entirely missing in the dataset was not considere
d by the original multiple imputation literature.
 The academic background for predicting an entirely missing variable in
 a given dataset is more aligned to the small area estimation literature
 (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "rao2005small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "rao2015small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 In model-based small area estimation auxiliary information from censuses,
 registers or other large surveys are leveraged to produce statistics with
 sufficient precision where survey data alone is not enough, for example
 small geographical areas.
 Perhaps the first instance of survey-to-survey imputation, as is applied
 in this paper, comes from the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 which is more aligned to small area estimation and noted by the authors.
 The key difference to small area estimation up to that point was that 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 predicted the variable of interest, consumption, at the household level
 and from that they obtained aggregate statistics based on the prediction
 of consumption.
 While earlier examples exist of combining data from different sources to
 predict household-level variables (e.g., Arellano and Meghir 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citebyear
key "arellano1992female"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), the innovation in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 lies in applying models estimated from survey data to census data to replicate
 the full consumption distribution.
 This approach allows for the estimation of poverty and other welfare indicators
 as if welfare data were available in the census itself.
 After refinements by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, the work became the basis of what later was referred to as poverty mapping
 in the World Bank.
 The key difference between survey-to-survey imputation and small area estimatio
n is that the latter aims to replicate the welfare distribution for each
 specific area or group of interest, rather than only for the entire population.
 One of the first applications of the methods from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to predict national level poverty can be seen in the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "simler2004estimating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 who use the methods to track changes in poverty in Mozambique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Survey-to-survey (S2S) imputation for poverty measurement relies on the
 assumption that a population's welfare distribution can be captured by
 a linear model which is estimated on the 
\begin_inset Formula $source$
\end_inset

 data.
 For simplicity, the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $target$
\end_inset

 is used to indicate when parameters are applied to the target data.
 Following the presentation by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, but ignoring the nested error component,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is later considered for robustness checks.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the population's assumed data generating process (DGP) for transformed
 welfare 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Transformed data is often used as the dependent variable to ensure the model's
 assumptions hold.
 For simplicity, throughout this document the assumed transformation is
 the natural logarithm, although many others are possible.
 Meeting the model's statistical assumptions is crucial for reliable estimation.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrate that violations of these assumptions, particularly the normality
 of residuals, can lead to biased poverty estimates.
 Their analysis shows that appropriate transformation of the dependent variable
 (household welfare) can significantly reduce such bias.
 When the standard logarithmic transformation proves insufficient, alternative
 transformations may be necessary to better approximate normality in the
 model's error term.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ln\,y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+e_{i};\:i=1,\ldots,N\label{eq:welfare model}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e\stackrel{i.i.d.}{\sim}N(0,\sigma_{e}^{2})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of independent variables common to the source survey and the
 target survey, and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a random disturbance term that is assumed to be distributed i.i.d.
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\sigma_{e}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 as well as the 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 parameters are estimated using the source survey or the training data since
 this is the only data where the welfare vector of interest (
\begin_inset Formula $\ln\,y_{i}$
\end_inset

) is available.
 These estimated parameters are then applied to the target data to predict
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln\,y_{i}$
\end_inset

, and from that poverty or other welfare related indicators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because normally distributed errors are assumed in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:welfare model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, although this can be relaxed as later illustrated, for any given household
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the target survey, the probability of being poor for household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is entirely dependent on its expected welfare, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\beta,$
\end_inset

 and the distribution of its idiosyncratic term, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is assumed to follow 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\sim N(0,σ_{e}^{2})$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Pr\left(poor_{i}=1\right)=Φ\,\left(\frac{\ln z-x_{i}\beta}{\sqrt{\sigma_{e}^{2}}}\right)\label{eq:cum prob}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln z$
\end_inset

 is the natural log of the chosen poverty threshold,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that if the transformation of the dependent variable is not the natural
 logarithm, then this is not valid.
 The poverty line must be transformed in a similar manner as the dependent
 variable.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Φ\left(.\right)$
\end_inset

 is the standard normal CDF.
 Consequently, what the method calculates is each household's probability
 of being poor.
 The average probability of being poor across households corresponds to
 the national poverty rate.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that because the only thing differing across households in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:cum prob"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 are the characteristics, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, a proxy means test (PMT) approach that relies only on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\beta$
\end_inset

 will yield the same household ranking as the survey-to-survey approach
 if the same model is used for either.
 Under a PMT, the threshold is chosen at a given percentile of 
\begin_inset Formula $x\beta$
\end_inset

 so that by construction it yields a desired proportion of people eligible.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implementation of welfare predictions to the target data can follow
 different approaches.
 The most common method, grounded in the multiple imputation literature,
 generates several welfare vectors to reflect prediction uncertainty.
 This approach is aligned to what was implemented in poverty mapping through
 the PovMap software (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "zhao2006user"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) and is similar to the methodology used in Stata's multiple imputation
 commands (
\family typewriter
mi regress
\family default
).
 Under this approach, each imputed welfare vector is generated through a
 five-step process:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Taken from 
\family typewriter
mi regress
\family default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "StataCorp2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Obtain model parameter estimates for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 via ordinary least squares fit on the training data (Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:welfare model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 ), noted below as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The distribution of the error terms is drawn from its posterior Chi-square
 distribution, where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is the number of observations in the training or source data which is sampled
 from 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is the number of covariates in the model, including the model intercept:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{e}^{2*}\sim\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}\frac{\left(n-K\right)}{\chi_{n-K}^{2}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
With 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2*}$
\end_inset

 in hand it is possible to update the variance-covariance matrix needed
 for drawing the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

 parameters: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta^{*}\sim MVN\left(\hat{\beta},\sigma_{e}^{2*}\left(X'X\right)^{-1}\right),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Then errors are drawn from:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e_{i}^{*}\sim N\left(0,\sigma_{e}^{2*}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Each imputed transformed welfare for household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $target$
\end_inset

 data under imputation 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 is given by: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ln y_{im}^{*}=x_{i_{target}}\beta_{m}^{*}+e_{im}^{*}\label{eq:MI}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Repeat steps 2 through 5 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 sets of imputed values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y_{i1}^{*},\ln y_{i2}^{*},\ldots,\ln y_{iM}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, for each imputed vector, a new 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2*}$
\end_inset

 is drawn and used to draw 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

 and a household specific residual, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The multiple imputation simulation approach is not necessarily aligned
 to reducing the prediction mean squared errors (MSE), but as noted by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 the purpose is to minimize the likelihood of false positives, since normally
 the imputed vectors are frequently used for regression analysis (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "rodas2021pull"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a discussion related to SAE).
 Alternatively, step 4 may be skipped and the imputed transformed welfare
 can be obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y_{im}^{*}\sim N\left(x_{i_{target}}\beta_{m}^{*},\sigma_{e}^{2*}\right).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An alternative approach, derived from the small area estimation literature,
 treats the source survey as the best representation of the true welfare
 distribution.
 Under this approach, parameters estimated from the source survey are applied
 directly to the target data through Monte Carlo simulation.
 For straightforward indicators like poverty rates, practitioners can simply
 apply the asymptotic formula for expected values (Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:cum prob"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 The steps are straight forward:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Fit Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:welfare model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 to the training data using ordinary least squares to obtain estimates of
 the model parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Each transformed welfare for household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the Monte Carlo simulation is given by – note how the coefficients remain
 constant across Monte Carlo simulations:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Monte Carlo simulations are not always necessary and for poverty one could
 immediately apply Eq.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:cum prob"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, for more complex parameters Monte Carlo simulations allow for
 indicators that are difficult to handle analytically.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ln y_{im}^{*}\sim N\left(x_{i_{target}}\hat{\beta},\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}\right)\label{eq:Fixed}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Repeat step 2 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 sets of imputed values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y_{i1}^{*},\ln y_{i2}^{*},\ldots,\ln y_{iM}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recent methodological advances have expanded model fitting options to include
 machine learning (ML) techniques.
 However, since most ML methods do not make explicit assumptions about error
 term distributions, they cannot directly replicate the multiple imputation
 approach described above.
 Nevertheless, bootstrap-based alternatives exist where draws from the empirical
 residuals from the source survey are used to obtain a welfare vector (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}^{*}=\hat{e}_{j},$
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $j\sim\mathrm{Uniform}\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$
\end_inset

).
 These were implemented in both PovMap (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "zhao2006user"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) and Stata's 
\family typewriter
sae 
\family default
command by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "nguyen2018sae"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This bootstrapping approach has proven particularly valuable for regularized
 regression techniques, as demonstrated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lucchetti2024application"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 with lasso regression,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://github.com/pcorralrodas/lassopmm"
target "https://github.com/pcorralrodas/lassopmm"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and may be extended to other ML methods such as gradient boosting, random
 forests, or Bayesian additive regression trees.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An additional method that can be paired with machine learning techniques
 as well as OLS, is predictive mean matching (PMM).
 The approach compares the predicted values from the target data to those
 from the training data, specifically by minimizing the absolute difference
 between linear predictors 
\begin_inset Formula $|x_{i_{target}}\hat{\beta}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}|$
\end_inset

.
 Once a specified number of nearest neighbors is identified, one of the
 actual observed welfare values is randomly drawn 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 PMM is notably less sensitive to model misspecification and is robust to
 back transformation (ibid).
 For instance, models estimated on 
\begin_inset Formula $\log(y)$
\end_inset

 typically yield similar results to those based on 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(y)$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), although differences in explained variance can still affect poverty estimates.
 The number of donors (i.e.
 neighbors) is determined by the practitioner.
 While setting this number to one can result in repeated values—especially
 when model fit is weak—
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "van2018flexible"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 recommends using five neighbors.
 He also emphasizes that this choice should be adaptive, depending on the
 sample size and the model’s explanatory power, as measured by its 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Assuming that equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:welfare model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 is fit via OLS and ignoring sampling weights, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 will be equal to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=(X'X)^{-1}X'y\label{eq:b1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
or, replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $X'\beta+e$
\end_inset

, one gets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=\beta+(X'X)^{-1}X'e\label{eq:b2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Imputing to a contemporaneous survey
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:sametime"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imputation of poverty to a contemporaneous survey can be a useful tool.
 For example, assume a country collects a living standard survey annually
 and has also collected a Demographic and Health Survey (DHS).
 If one would like to determine how nutritional indicators among children
 vary across the monetary welfare distribution, survey to survey imputation
 may be of use to impute a monetary welfare aggregate to the DHS to determine
 what share of children in the poorest quantiles are stunted.
 Nevertheless, practitioners could also impute the indicator of interest
 to the living standards survey.
 For example, with the goal of determining energy poverty in Bulgaria, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rude2024statistical"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 impute energy spending from the household budget survey (HBS) to the European
 Survey of Income and Living Conditions to determine profiles of the energy
 poor.
 These settings resemble the “no single dataset contains all variables”
 problem discussed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rubin1986statistical"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, where imputations may be performed in either direction to enable inference.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fundamental limitation, however, is that when key variables such as stunting
 and welfare are never jointly observed, their conditional association,
 given the common covariates used for matching, is not identified from the
 observed data.
 As a result, identifying stunting-by-welfare gradients requires an additional,
 inherently untestable restriction (most commonly conditional independence)
 or a sensitivity analysis that varies the assumed association between stunting
 and welfare (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "Raessler2004DataFusion"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The successful application of survey-to-survey imputation depends on meeting
 several preconditions, first outlined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "jesko1998"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and later refined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These preconditions ensure that the fundamental assumption – both surveys
 represent the same underlying population – holds.
 Two contemporaneous surveys which represent the same population are needed
 for the approach to work well.
 In this instance, the assumption of stable parameters does not affect the
 method's estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, covariate comparability: the predictors used to impute welfare must
 be available in both surveys and defined and measured in a consistent way.
 This extends beyond the presence of similarly named variables to encompass
 harmonized concepts, response categories, and field protocols; differences
 in definitions (for example, how household membership is determined) can
 alter both the distribution of covariates and their relationship to welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, distributional alignment: the marginal distributions of key predictors
 should be similar across the source and target surveys, not only in means
 but also in dispersion.
 Large discrepancies may indicate that the target survey places substantial
 weight on regions of the covariate space with limited support in the source
 survey, undermining the model’s ability to replicate the welfare distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Third, structural coherence: the joint relationships among predictors—and,
 by implication, the mapping from predictors to welfare—should be broadly
 consistent across surveys.
 In a contemporaneous setting, parameter instability over time is not the
 primary concern; rather, the key risk is that survey design differences
 or measurement inconsistency of covariates shift underlying associations
 in ways that make the welfare model non-portable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Model Assumptions 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reliability of welfare predictions in the same period depends on meeting
 the model's statistical assumptions.
 Conditional on comparability and alignment across surveys, the quality
 of welfare imputations hinges on the adequacy of the statistical model
 used to generate predicted welfare (
\begin_inset Formula $x'\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

) and simulated disturbances (
\begin_inset Formula $e^{*}$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Three assumptions are particularly salient.
 First, many implementations rely on approximately normal residuals; when
 this is violated, transformations of the welfare aggregate can improve
 approximation to normality (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "corral2022guidelines"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 If transformations remain insufficient, an alternative is to draw disturbances
 from the empirical residual distribution, though this requires additional
 structure—most notably that residuals are appropriately centered and, in
 common implementations, approximately symmetric.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Although OLS residuals are mean-zero in sample, symmetry matters when residuals
 are used as a stand-in for the unobserved component in simulation.
 If the residual distribution is skewed, drawing errors unconditionally
 from the empirical residuals can systematically alter the estimated probabiliti
es.
 If large positive values are more likely to be sampled than large negative
 values, or vice versa, it is consequential for poverty prediction.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, the model often assumes homoscedastic errors; this can be relaxed
 by explicitly modeling heteroskedasticity, as in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2002micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "harvey1976estimating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Third, the mapping from covariates to welfare is frequently treated as
 linear, but can be made more flexible through richer parametric specifications
 (e.g., polynomial terms and interactions) or through nonparametric and machine-le
arning estimators such as random forests or gradient boosting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Imputing across time
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Survey-to-survey (S2S) imputation is promoted as a valuable tool for filling
 gaps in welfare data, but its use over time requires greater caution than
 for contemporaneous applications as discussed in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Survey-to-Survey-Imputation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 The method assumes that relationships estimated in a given period can be
 mapped to a different point in time.
 The core assumption—that changes in welfare reflect only shifts in observable
 characteristics, with stable relationships between covariates and welfare—becom
es increasingly fragile as the time between surveys grows.
 Structural economic change, evolving labor markets, and shifting household
 behaviors can all violate this assumption, leading to imputation errors
 that distort actual welfare dynamics.
 Moreover, S2S methods tend to reproduce the welfare distribution of the
 source survey: coefficients capture the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 from the source period, and errors are drawn from that same model in the
 source data.
 As a result, households with identical characteristics in the source and
 target periods are assigned identical poverty probabilities, regardless
 of contextual changes.
 Because the method assumes stable parameters, it cannot reflect how the
 poverty risk of households with similar observed characteristics may change
 over time, which limits its ability to recover true changes in poverty
 and inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, omitted variables can be particularly consequential when imputing
 across time.
 In contemporaneous settings, these concerns are sometimes viewed as second-orde
r for prediction because, in the estimation sample, OLS residuals are mean-zero
 and the intercept absorbs the average influence of unobservables, so fitted
 values can perform well in reproducing mean welfare (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "a4f1365d-ff19-34b5-b1b5-15ec4c1e5f15,elbers2003micro,dang2025poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "dang2025poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (2025 p9) states that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

worries about endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 pose far less important, if any, concerns in our context.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 An endogenous variable is frequently defined as an explanatory variable
 that may be correlated with the error term (Wooldridge, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citebyear
key "wooldridge2009introductory"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 p88).
 Omitted variables are related as these are correlated to the error term
 and a covariate.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This rationale is less compelling in cross-time applications, where the
 distribution of unobserved determinants may shift between the source and
 target periods due to shocks, policy changes, or structural transformation.
 In that case, source-period parameters can embed period-specific correlations
 between observables and unobservables that are not portable, implying biased
 welfare and poverty predictions even when covariates are measured consistently.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See Annex 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Omitted-Variables"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for an exposition.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These issues are instances of a broader concern: parameter instability.
 Cross-time S2S applies both 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and the residual structure (e.g., 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 or a modeled heteroskedastic variance) estimated historically to a different
 period.
 When returns evolve—as emphasized in decompositions that allow coefficients
 to change over time (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "bourguignon2008beyond"
literal "false"

\end_inset

)—imputations may primarily reflect compositional change rather than the
 true evolution of the welfare distribution.
 This can be seen from 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=(X'X)^{-1}X'y$
\end_inset

: substantial shifts in the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 change both 
\begin_inset Formula $X'X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X'y$
\end_inset

, with no guarantee that the implied mapping remains valid in the target
 period.
 In practice, cross-time reliability can be undermined by changes in determinant
s and returns, sampling frames,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Changes in sampling frame are not necessarily problematic, as long as the
 data is still representative of the population of interest.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 policy regimes, macroeconomic shocks, and survey design or implementation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, these limitations are not resolved by selecting models solely to
 optimize in-sample fit (e.g., via stepwise selection or regularization).
 Portability depends on the stability of the conditional relationship being
 transported; if regressors proxy for unobserved determinants whose correlation
 structure changes over time, relationships that are predictive within-period
 can become unreliable out-of-period.
 Given the growing reliance on S2S to monitor poverty in the absence of
 frequent welfare aggregates, cross-time applications should be interpreted
 cautiously and, where feasible, triangulated against independent signals
 of welfare dynamics—such as national accounts aggregates, labor-market
 indicators, price statistics, or relevant administrative series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model-Based Simulations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section builds on the concepts introduced in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-basics-behind"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 by examining how violations of key survey-to-survey (S2S) assumptions translate
 into bias and instability in imputed poverty estimates.
 The core analysis relies on simulated data, which provides a controlled
 environment in which individual features of the data-generating process
 (DGP) can be perturbed in isolation.
 This design allows the analysis to attribute changes in performance to
 specific assumption failures—something that is difficult to do with observation
al data, where multiple sources of non-comparability and model misspecification
 tend to move together.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To strengthen the external relevance of the simulation findings, two robustness
 extensions are introduced.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We thank anonymous reviewers for these recommendations.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 First, an alternative DGP is considered in which the “linear” component
 of the welfare model (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\beta$
\end_inset

) is far away from normally distributed, providing a stricter test of approaches
 that rely on distributional assumptions.
 Second, the analysis uses empirical covariate structure from Mexico following
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

: the linear predictor is estimated on the Mexican intracensal data and
 then combined with simulated error processes that vary in form—(i) nested
 normal errors consistent with clustered disturbance structures, (ii) heterosked
astic errors, and (iii) non-normal errors generated from a Student’s 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 Together, these exercises are intended to evaluate whether the paper’s
 recommendations remain robust when residual behavior departs from the baseline
 assumptions in ways that are empirically plausible in household survey
 settings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Model-based simulations thus serve a dual purpose in what follows: they
 provide a transparent framework for stress-testing S2S imputation under
 targeted assumption violations, and they support more defensible guidance
 by demonstrating which findings persist across alternative DGPs and empirically
 grounded error structures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Creating populations
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Creating-populations"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
DGP 1 - approximately normal 
\begin_inset Formula $x\beta$
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:DGP-1--"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We create 500 synthetic populations of 20,000 households where the welfare
 of the population is generated with the following data generating process
 (DGP): 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ln y_{i}=3+0.1x_{1_{i}}+0.5x_{2_{i}}-0.25x_{3_{i}}+0.2x_{4_{i}}-0.15x_{5_{i}}+e_{i}\label{eq:dgp_model}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\sim N(0,0.5^{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is a discrete variable, simulated as the rounded integer value of the maximum
 between 1 and a random Poisson variable with mean 
\begin_inset Formula $λ=4$
\end_inset

.
 It is rounded to create a variable similar to the often used household
 size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, taking value 1 when a random uniform number between
 0 and 1 is less than 0.2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, taking value 1 when a random uniform number between
 0 and 1 is less than 0.5 as long as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=1$
\end_inset

, otherwise it is equal to 0
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{4}\sim N(2.5,2^{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{5}$
\end_inset

 is a variable drawn from a Student’s t distribution with 5 degrees of freedom
 and scaled by 0.25
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This DGP yields a Gini coefficient of approximately 0.38, and the covariates
 explain about 47 percent of the variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As an additional robustness check, Annex 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:DGP_cluster"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 considers an alternative DGP which follows an assumed double nested error
 model similar to that of small area estimation based on 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ea7f7e4f-7381-39b0-abdd-8da14c150408"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, while maintaining the covariate-generation process above.
 This extension is used to illustrate that, when the true DGP includes clustered
 disturbances, ignoring the nesting primarily affects inference and uncertainty
 rather than systematically shifting bias and MSE in mean poverty predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
DGP 2 - non-normally distributed 
\begin_inset Formula $x\beta$
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:DGP-2--"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A second DGP modifies the construction of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{5}$
\end_inset

 to generate an 
\begin_inset Formula $X\beta$
\end_inset

 that departs from normality, while keeping the generation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\:x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 unchanged.
 Additionally, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\sim N(0,0.3^{2})$
\end_inset

 is also adjusted to ensure the covariates explain roughly 43 percent of
 the variation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y_{i}$
\end_inset

 while yielding a Gini coefficient of roughly 0.23.
 Specifically, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{5}$
\end_inset

 are simulated as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

 is a categorical variable, that is simulated as the rounded integer value
 of the maximum between 1 and a random Poisson variable with mean 
\begin_inset Formula $λ=5$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{4}$
\end_inset

 indicates low values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

 and is equal to 1 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}<3$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{5}$
\end_inset

 indicates high values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

 and is equal to 1 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\geq8$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

 is not explicitly part of the model.
 Following the previous section, a double nested error model structure is
 also considered for this DGP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
DGP relying on real world data
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:DGP-Mex"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To complement the stylized simulations, the paper also evaluates robustness
 using covariate structure estimated from real world data.
 Specifically, the publicly available Mexican Intercensus Survey of 2015,
 carried out by the Mexican National Institute of Statistics and Geography
 (INEGI), is used.
 The survey consists of 5.9 million households and is representative across
 2,457 municipalities of the country and localities with a population larger
 than 50,000 people.
 It also includes an income measure of welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The analysis uses the processed data described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2025poverty"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 There, the original intercensus data were modified to randomly remove 90
 percent of households that did not report income, additionally all municipaliti
es with less than 500 households sampled were removed.
 The final census consists of 3.9 million households across 1,865 municipalities.
 From this frame, 501 survey samples of roughly 23,540 households each are
 drawn, with 10 households per PSU, reflecting design features common in
 multi-stage household surveys 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "grosh1996manual"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Each sample includes all 32 states and between 951 and 1,020 municipalities.
 Further details on the sampling strategy are provided in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To construct the experimental data used here, a 10 percent simple random
 sample across all PSUs in the census is taken.
 This subsample is used to estimate a linear model of log income using the
 full set of eligible household- and municipality-level covariates, following
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The resulting model's linear fit (
\begin_inset Formula $X\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

) is kept fixed, but different disturbances are added to generate 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln y$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Nested normal error model: independent normally distributed disturbances
 at the PSU, municipality, and household levels, calibrated to the estimated
 variance components in the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Non normal errors: error terms are drawn from a Student's 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution with 8 degrees of freedom, scaled to ensure an SD of roughly
 0.8.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Multiplicative heteroskedasticity: A covariate, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{het}\sim N(0.5,0.5^{2})$
\end_inset

, is simulated.
 Then conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{het}$
\end_inset

 the error terms are drawn: 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{het}\sim N\left(0,\sigma^{2}(x_{het})\right),$
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=\frac{\exp\left((1/6)x_{het}\right)}{1.368}$
\end_inset

 to ensure that the model's RMSE is roughly 0.8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The observed welfare variable is not used directly in these experiments.
 The purpose of the Mexican exercise is not to reproduce Mexico-specific
 welfare results, but to anchor the simulations in an empirically realistic
 covariate structure while retaining control over the error-generating process.
 This design isolates the performance consequences of the disturbances noted
 above in a setting with realistic covariate distributions and sampling
 features, avoiding conclusions that would otherwise be driven by country-
 and period-specific features of the observed welfare aggregate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Evaluation metrics 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Evaluation-metrics"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main performance metrics are bias and mean squared error (MSE).
 Under the DGPs described in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:DGP-1--"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:DGP-2--"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, and unless otherwise noted, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=500$
\end_inset

 populations 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(b=1,\ldots,B\right)$
\end_inset

 are generated.
 For each population 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, the imputation model is estimated on a training sample and applied to
 a separate target sample to produce an estimate of the indicator of interest,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 (head count monetary poverty in this work).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Empirical bias and MSE are computed across replications as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Bias\left(\tau\right)=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\left(\hat{\tau}_{b}-\tau_{b}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MSE\left(\tau\right)=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}\left(\hat{\tau}_{b}-\tau_{b}\right)^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{b}$
\end_inset

 denotes the true population value under population 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tau}_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the corresponding estimate obtained by fitting the model on the training
 sample and predicting welfare in the target sample.
 Note that under this process, following the assumed DGP, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is a random variable (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 p376).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the Mexican data, the validation design differs slightly.
 The population parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is treated as fixed (observed in the census-based file), and repeated survey
 samples are drawn from this same population.
 A model is first estimated in a randomly selected training sample and then
 applied to 
\begin_inset Formula $S=500$
\end_inset

 target samples drawn from the population.
 In the small area estimation literature, this approach is commonly referred
 to as design-based validation (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "tzavidis2018start,corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Empirical design-based bias and MSE are computed as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Bias\left(\tau\right)=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\left(\hat{\tau}_{s}-\tau\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MSE\left(\tau\right)=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s=1}^{S}\left(\hat{\tau}_{s}-\tau\right)^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 denotes the true population value as observed in the census and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tau}_{s}$
\end_inset

 is the corresponding estimate obtained by applying the training-sample
 model to target sample 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $s=1,\ldots,S$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How to Impute?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The typical imputation approach undertaken for S2S work done to predict
 poverty follows the literature on multiple imputation (MI).
 A similar method was followed in the original software implementation of
 small area estimation proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, PovMap (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "zhao2006user"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Under the MI approach, the parameters estimated on the training data are
 not applied directly to the target data, instead the parameters to be applied
 to the target data are drawn from their posterior distributions as illustrated
 in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-basics-behind"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 For small area estimation, the imputation approach from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elbers2003micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 has been updated to follow the approach from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See a detailed discussion in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "rodas2021pull,corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Under the method of the latter, the parameters estimated using the training
 data are applied directly to the data and noise of the predicted indicator
 is estimated via a parametric bootstrap which is aligned to the model's
 assumptions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gonzalez-manteiga2008bootstrap"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To compare different imputation methods, a simple random sample (SRS) comprising
 20 percent of the data generated in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:DGP-1--"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 is used as both source and target data.
 In the results presented in this section that use the simulated data, the
 same dataset is used for both model development and prediction.
 This approach eliminates potential additional noise introduced by sampling
 variability, allowing for a more controlled assessment of imputation performanc
e that is entirely driven by the model fit and its assumptions.
 The process is applied across 500 generated populations allowing us to
 assess the method's empirical bias.
 Additionally, where noted, results using the Mexican data are presented.
 Under these simulations, models are estimated in a given sample and then
 applied across 500 samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Why simulate the errors?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Survey-to-survey (S2S) imputation of poverty is not simply about predicting
 the dependent variable.
 Relying solely on the predicted value of welfare to derive poverty rates
 often yields poor results.
 This section uses the synthetic population introduced in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Creating-populations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 to illustrate this point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We fit a linear model on a simple random sample (SRS) comprising 20 percent
 of the population.
 This model is then used to generate two sets of predicted values for the
 full data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The linear prediction, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y_{i}}=x_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The linear prediction plus a randomly drawn error: 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}=x_{i}\hat{\beta}+e_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is drawn from 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\hat{\sigma}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:why_errors"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 shows that relying solely on the linear prediction, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\hat{\beta},$
\end_inset

 fails to replicate the true distribution of welfare.
 For example, if the true poverty line is 2.39—corresponding to a 20 percent
 poverty rate in the actual data—then the linear prediction alone would
 imply a poverty rate of only 10 percent.
 This gap persists despite the fact that the linear prediction has a lower
 mean squared error (MSE = 0.25 for both DGP) than the version with stochastic
 error (MSE = 0.5 for both DGP).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The MSE here is measured as the mean squared difference in predictions across
 all households.
 For the former the difference is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}\right)$
\end_inset

 and for the latter the difference is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}-e_{i}^{*}\right).$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This suggests that minimizing MSE of predicted welfare is not sufficient
 for reproducing the distributional properties needed to estimate poverty
 accurately.
 Moreover, the Spearman rank correlation is higher for the linear prediction
 (
\begin_inset Formula $x\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 vs.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

) than for the version with added error (
\begin_inset Formula $x\hat{\beta}+e^{*}$
\end_inset

 vs.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

).
 In other words, correctly ranking households is also not enough to replicate
 the full welfare distribution or derive accurate poverty statistics.
 
\end_layout

