\begin_layout Subsubsection
Under a normal error DGP
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:normal error dgp"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The baseline imputation methods assume that welfare is linearly related
 to a set of characteristics and that errors follow a two-fold double nested
 normal error.
 The main results are based on a normal double nested error distribution
 as illustrated in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:DGP-Mex"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Varying the number of imputations (20 versus 100) has virtually no effect
 on bias or MSE (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:bias_eb_mi"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 However, the MI approach yields estimates that are both more biased and
 noisier than approaches that treat model parameters as fixed—namely the
 OLS and nested-error implementations in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Fixed"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 These fixed-parameter predictions follow the small area estimation approach
 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and variance model parameters are kept fixed..
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, ignoring the clustered nature of the data has a minimal impact
 on the estimates' empirical bias and MSE.
 Point estimates that ignore the nested structure (OLS) perform similarly
 to, and in this case slightly better than, estimates that explicitly model
 location effects (nested).
 In small area estimation, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ea7f7e4f-7381-39b0-abdd-8da14c150408"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 suggest that modeling cluster effects at a level misaligned with the level
 of reporting can bias noise estimates.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 reinforce this point using model-based simulations but show that the bias
 of point estimates remains unaffected.
 In our case, because the results reported here are at the national level,
 omitting random location effects does not materially reduce the validity
 or precision of the poverty estimates.
 These results are similar under the alternative DGPs described in section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:DGP-1--"
plural "false"
