.
 Each simulated population contains 20,000 observations.
 Within each population, a random 30 percent subsample is designated as
 the target survey and an independent 20 percent subsample as the source
 survey used for model estimation (the two samples are drawn separately
 and may overlap).
 This procedure is repeated for 
\begin_inset Formula $B=10,000$
\end_inset

 bootstrap populations.
 For each population, new errors are drawn from the assumed error distribution;
 poverty is then predicted under each population using (i) MI, with uncertainty
 computed via Rubin’s rules, and (ii) fixed-parameter prediction with uncertaint
y computed via a parametric bootstrap 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gonzalez-manteiga2008bootstrap"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Thus, under each population a noise estimate is obtained for each method.
 The resulting average estimated variance/MSE from each method across the
 10,000 generated populations is compared to the empirical MSE across population
s:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MSE=\sum_{b=1}\frac{\left(\hat{\tau}_{b}-\tau_{b}\right)^{2}}{B}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the true population indicator for population 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tau}_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the predicted indicator for population 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 following MI or fixed parameters.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Both methods here appear to overestimate the empirical MSE.
 The reason for the overestimation is that both methods measure the noise
 based on the target survey which is smaller than the population.
 Since the MSE is equal to the sum of the squared bias and the variance
 of the indicator of interest and both methods are nearly unbiased (with
 slightly larger bias under MI), differences are mostly driven by the variance
 term, which is mechanically larger when evaluated using the smaller target
 sample (see Annex 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:sample_size_mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

