\begin_layout Subsubsection
Omitted Variables 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Omitted-Variables"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Omitted variables and endogeneity have traditionally not been major concerns
 in predictions such as S2S or small area estimation.
 In a contemporaneous setting, OLS fitted values remain unbiased for the
 conditional mean under standard conditions, with unobserved determinants
 absorbed—on average—by the intercept and by coefficients on correlated
 regressors.
 This reassurance is weaker in cross-time applications: if the distribution
 of omitted determinants changes between the source and target periods,
 the source-period relationship between observables and welfare need not
 remain transferable, and prediction errors can become systematic.
 For a more detailed discussion, see the annex (Annex 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:ann_ovb"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a plausible application of survey-to-survey (S2S) imputation for
 monetary poverty in a conflict-affected rural setting.
 Suppose conflict declines between two survey rounds and, as a result, poverty
 falls.
 If the imputation model does not control for conflict directly, it may
 rely on fast-changing consumption indicators—such as whether households
 consumed meat, eggs, or chocolate—as proxies for welfare dynamics, consistent
 with guidance in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yoshida2022concept"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However, if conflict simultaneously depresses consumption of these goods
 and reduces household expenditure, then omitting conflict induces omitted-varia
ble bias: the coefficients on the consumption indicators partly reflect
 the conflict channel, and changes in conflict over time can translate into
 biased predictions of welfare and poverty (refer to Annex 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:ann_ovb"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proxy variables can, in principle, mitigate omitted-variable bias 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "wooldridge2009introductory,wooldridge2010econometric,YoshidaKawashimaTakamatsu2025ShockResponsiveness"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, but not every correlated covariate qualifies as a valid proxy.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "wooldridge2009introductory"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, for example, discusses “ability” as an omitted determinant of wages and
 notes that while education is correlated with ability, it is itself a structura
lly relevant regressor in the wage equation and therefore should not be
 treated as a proxy; he instead uses IQ as a proxy for ability.
 Formally, a valid proxy must satisfy two conditions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "wooldridge2009introductory,wooldridge2010econometric"
literal "false"

\end_inset

: (i) it must be strongly correlated with the omitted factor, and (ii) it
 must be conditionally irrelevant for the outcome once the omitted factor
 is included—i.e., if the omitted variable were observed, the proxy would
 add no explanatory power to the mode.
 Item-specific consumption indicators may plausibly satisfy the first condition,
 but they are unlikely to satisfy the second because they are mechanically
 related to household expenditure because household expenditure is a function
 of the goods it has decided to purchase, and thus remain directly relevant
 determinants of the welfare aggregate, the dependent variable.
 In other words, a valid proxy should be related to expenditures only through
 its relationship with the omitted variable.
 When this “conditional irrelevance” requirement fails, the proposed proxy
 is simply another regressor that can inherit bias when it is correlated
 with the omitted factor, potentially amplifying—rather than alleviating—predict
ion error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simulate the potential impact of omitted variable bias on poverty predictions
 over time the data simulated in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Creating-populations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 is expanded to include 2 new covariates: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Conflict.
 Conflict is assumed to be negatively correlated with welfare with a coefficient
 equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.3$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
It is simulated as a binary variable taking value 1 when a random uniform
 number between 0 and 1 is less than 
\begin_inset Formula $c=0.4$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, it is expected that 40 percent of households are affected by conflict.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Eggs purchased.
 The variable is assumed to be positively correlated to welfare with a coefficie
nt equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $0.4$
\end_inset

.
 However, the share of households purchasing eggs can change due to conflict
 and external factors unrelated to welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
The variable is simulated as binary, taking the value 1 if a randomly drawn
 uniform number between 0 and 1 falls below 
\begin_inset Formula $0.5x-0.5\times Conflict$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x=1$
\end_inset

 in the baseline.
 Importantly, “eggs purchased” is not generated from a behavioral demand
 model.
 It is introduced as a reduced-form indicator whose probability shifts with
 conflict and with an exogenous component 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 that captures non-welfare related drivers of purchase decisions—such as
 market access and availability, supply disruptions, relative price changes,
 or shifts in preferences.
 The objective is to mimic a setting in which an item-level consumption
 indicator moves because the economic environment changes along with changes
 in conflict.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Under the baseline simulation, the value for 
\begin_inset Formula $x=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c=0.4$
\end_inset

, thus, roughly 40 percent of households are affected by conflict and roughly
 30 percent of households purchased eggs.
 Welfare is simulated assuming the true DGP, and thus includes conflict
 and eggs with their true coefficients.
 The prediction model is produced omitting conflict and is executed using
 the complete data to avoid other potential sources of bias (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Omitted-variable-model"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 As presented in Annex 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:ann_ovb"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the coefficient on eggs is upward biased.
 Due to the omission of conflict, the constant term of the regression is
 downward biased.
 The adjustment in the constant term ensures that the model's prediction
 of the dependent variable is unbiased.
 As can be seen, omitting conflict from the model also leads to a larger
 RMSE than the truth, which will likely affect poverty predictions over
 time.
 Nevertheless, for predictions to the same period OVB has little if any
 impact (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:ovb same period"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The reason for this is that even in the presence of OVB 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{var}\left[x\hat{\beta}\right]+\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 will be the same as the one where conflict is present and since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is also equal poverty predictions in the same period are unaffected.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

