\begin_layout Standard
Once conflict subsides, so that the share of households exposed to conflict
 is only 20 percent, the share of households purchasing eggs will increase
 to 40 percent due to the assumed relationship.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $x=1$
\end_inset

in this scenario, thus there is no external shock to the egg purchase decision
 beyond conflict.
 Using the adjusted covariates, the true resulting welfare is calculated
 – including conflict and the correct coefficients.
 Then the model parameters obtained from the original data, excluding conflict,
 are used to obtain predictions of poverty across the welfare distribution.
 As can be seen, even in the absence of any other changes to the DGP, the
 predictions under the model will be considerably biased only due to a change
 in conflict (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:ovb_Dconflict_noDegg"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

