\begin_layout Subsection
Sample Size and MSE
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:sample_size_mse"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The MSE of an estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is defined as (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 p376):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MSE\left(\hat{\tau}\right)=Bias\left(\hat{\tau}\right)^{2}+\mathrm{var\left(\hat{\tau}\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Under the assumed DGP the target parameter is a random variable (ibid.).
 The usual decomposition of the MSE into variance and bias does not hold.
 However, as noted by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "molina2010small"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 it can be decomposed as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MSE\left(\hat{\tau}\right)=\mathrm{var}\left(\hat{\tau}-\tau\right)+\left\{ E\left(\hat{\tau}-\tau\right)\right\} ^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Under the imputation methods used in the examples, the bias 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(E\left(\hat{\tau}-\tau\right)\right)$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be 0 (ibid).
 Thus, the MSE is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{\mathrm{var}\left(\hat{\tau}-\tau\right)},$
\end_inset

 which is equal to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{var}\left(\bar{\tau}\right)=\mathrm{var}\left(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\tau_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathrm{var}\left(\bar{\tau}\right)=\frac{1}{N^{2}}\mathrm{var}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}\tau_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Consequently, the variance of an indicator is dependent on the true finite
 population's size.
 However, since the methods assess the variance based on the target data
 (6,000) and not the true population (20,000) the estimated variance will
 be larger than the true population variance which leads to a larger estimate
 of the MSE.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


